{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>A wise man's heart.</b> It does not refer to the physical heart, for it is in the middle for everyone, for the wise and for the fool, even though his head inclines toward the left side. Its meaning is that the intellect of the wise is with him, and at the time of need he finds it immediately in all his affairs, swiftly. And the opposite is the fool — as though a wise man's heart is at his right, for the right has more power than the left, and more swiftness, because the source of the blood, which is the liver, is toward the right side. And no left-handed person may object, for the parable follows what is found among the majority of people."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:49.065032317Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}